{# Contextual query resolution: what the user actually asked, in words that stand alone.

   Rendered only on turns the cheap gate in backend/chat/resolution.py could not settle
   locally — a message that carries its own subject never reaches this prompt.

   The output is consumed as a structured object, so this text defines the JUDGEMENTS
   only; the field descriptions on the pydantic schema define the shape. Stating the
   shape here as well would pay for it twice on every call.

   Whitespace: trim_blocks is on, so the newline after every {% %} tag is swallowed. #}
Rewrite the user's latest message as a question that stands on its own.

{% if persona %}
The assistant they are talking to: {{ persona }}
{% endif %}

The conversation so far:
{{ history }}
{% if hitl_prompt %}

The assistant then asked the user to clarify:
{{ hitl_prompt }}
{% if hitl_options %}
It offered these options:
{% for option in hitl_options %}
  - {{ option }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

The user's latest message:
{{ question }}

Produce a `question` that someone with no access to the conversation above could still search for and answer. Everything the message refers to indirectly — "this", "these years", "it", "the same", a bare "and what about...?" — must be replaced by what it actually points at, taken from the conversation.

Keep the user's own subject. You are resolving references, not improving the question: do not widen it, do not add a topic they did not raise, and do not answer it.

Write the question in the same language the user wrote in.

`constraints` are the conditions that carried over from earlier turns and still bind this answer — an age or grade range, a campus, a gender, a year, a document type. List each as a short phrase ("grades up to Year 6", "girls only", "the 2025/2026 year"). These are the difference between an answer about what was asked and an answer about everything the corpus holds, so a condition the user set earlier and has not withdrawn belongs here even when their latest message does not repeat it. Leave the list empty when the question binds nothing.

Classify the message as exactly one `intent`:

  standalone  — it already carries its own subject and needs nothing from the conversation.
  followup    — it continues the current subject, inheriting it and any conditions still in force. A bare answer to something the assistant asked ("grade 5", "he is 9") is a followup.
  correction  — it REPLACES how the assistant read the previous message, rather than adding to it. "no, I meant the fees", "not the uniform — the timings". The old reading is wrong and must be discarded, not combined with the new one.
  new_topic   — it changes the subject. Nothing from the conversation carries over, and `constraints` should be empty.

Worked examples, for a school knowledge base:

  earlier: "what is the uniform for children under year 6"
  now:     "and what is the fees for this years"
  -> question: "what are the school fees for children in the years up to Year 6"
     constraints: ["grades up to Year 6"]
     intent: followup

  earlier: "what is the uniform for children under year 6"
  asked:   "Which of these are you asking about?"
  now:     "no i mean what is the school fees for this years"
  -> question: "what are the school fees for the years up to Year 6"
     constraints: ["grades up to Year 6"]
     intent: correction

  earlier: "what are the fees for grade 3"
  now:     "who is the principal"
  -> question: "who is the principal"
     constraints: []
     intent: new_topic
